A||||18||daveml66||why do fiero clutches  SUCK!||||5||
Z||000000||daveml66||03-15-2001||06:03 PM||||87 just installed  4spd   2.8   with prolems.<BR>sometimes my clutch seems to work  pretty good then sometime it won't. like almost exactly 50% of the time its shift into gears and the other 50% it'll just grind.  i've bleed the system , i have the steel pedal.  could it be the  front cylinder that the pedal connects too  could replacing it help nething i have one on had that i know  works. any comments?||||reg||5
Z||000001||ChadMan||03-15-2001||06:40 PM||||If your clutch won't disengage properly, you know the pres.plate/TO bearing/clutch disc are good, and the pedal isn't bent, the clutch master cylinder is the next most likely place to look.  I found out the hard way that if the internal seals in the master cylinder are leaking, the clutch will bleed bubble-free and still not work worth a damn.<P>I have a NAPA clutch master cylinder on my car that has been great for 55K miles and counting.  It was a pretty easy job, and I'm no master mechanic.<P>Good luck!<p>[This message has been edited by ChadMan (edited 03-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||johnt671||03-15-2001||07:22 PM||||i had a clutch put in my 84 4cyl 4spd and also a new slave cyl.  when i picked the car up i couldn't shift unless i double cluched.  turned out the new slave cyl was bad.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||daveml66||03-16-2001||09:12 AM||||does anybody have a manual trans  fiero  that actually  WORKS   please someone give me some hope.. i just got thru reading the clutch info uncovered thread and it was almost depressing||||reg||9
Z||000004||fiero56||03-16-2001||09:42 AM||||I own two Fieros. One v-6 5-speed, and one v-6 4-speed. I have never had a clutch related problem yet. <P>When I replaced the clutch on my Formula, I decided to buy new master and slave cylinders before my 150K mile factory units failed. Here I am 20,000 miles later still no trouble. I haven't had to anything with my 4-speed yet. <BR>Maybe I'm just lucky, but I've never had clutch issues, other than that ATF thing, which doesn't count becuase I didn't follow factory recommendation, never do that again...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||03-16-2001||09:55 AM||||My 1986 GT 5 speed with 155,000 miles on the clutch hydraulics (clutch is about 3 years old) shifts fine, other than the fact that the 3rd gear synchro is gone... ||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||03-16-2001||10:02 AM||||use DOT5.1 fluid.. the DOT 4 has a much lower boiling point and that what causes most of the clutch related problems.  My SE had a hydraulic problem till i changed to the dot 5.1 from AGIP. a NAPA brand is now available for much les $. GT Bastard had a heat failure of the seals on the slave.. (headers just cooked the slave) he's replaced it and it seems fine again...||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tigger||03-16-2001||10:28 AM||||After a month or so after I bought my Fiero the clutch came apart.  It never gave me any warning that it was going away.  Went to take off from an intersection, and as I let the clutch out more and more, it just disintegrated.  Sounded like, "clang clang, grind grind, ugh ugh, poof"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> (Well, something like that)<P>Have no problems with the new one.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||stimpy||03-16-2001||10:43 AM||||*knocking on wood* The PO of my 86 GT replaced the clutch master cylinder at 124k miles. I have 148k mils now, and other then my clutch  being replaced with a slippery Borg-Warner when I had my engine rebuilt at 137k, I have no clutch issues.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Carrolles||03-16-2001||12:45 PM||||My 85SE v-6 4spd Master cylinder went out at 120000 miles.  I rebuilt it with an AutoZone seal kit.  The next week the slave went out.  Couldn't find a kit so I got a new one from CarPort.  Haven't had any problems yet after 10000 miles.<P>Ed Parks warned me that when one cylinder goes the other usually follows within a week are two.<P>I followed Ed's procedure for bleeding the hydraulics and everything is fine.<P>There is hope.<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000010||johnt671||03-16-2001||04:07 PM||||when i got mine (84 SE 4 spd) the guy that sold it to me said he put a new clutch in. i was happy as he owned a tranny shop and had a good rep in the area. i was on rt 80 in n.j., and heard a spring snapping sound and lost the clutch. he replaced the clutch for 100 bucks and two months later the same thing.  went to another shop and it turned out he hadn't put a pilot shaft bearing in.  three years later i'm still going and other than a cranky shift once in a while, no problems.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Richard Parnell||03-16-2001||06:17 PM||||I too had plenty of problems with the clutch not disengaging when I had the 4 cyl in my car.  When we put in the V8 I changed nothing in the tranny except of course the clutch, pressure plate, throwout bearing which was pretty new anyway.  We cleaned and lubed the shaft the bearing rides on.  I did nothing to the master and slave, didn't even bleed them again.  When we put the engine in all hooked up, the tranny shifted perfectly.  Actually it disengages much higher up on the clutch pedal throw than it ever did with the 4cly and Fiero clutch, which also wasn't that old.  Answer? I'd say changing to a better clutch like the centerforce (if they make them for the Fiero engine setup would be the answer. Just my experience with the whole thing.  I thought for sure I would be having the same crappy clutch performance with the V8 but I haven't had any problem and it shifts smooth as can be.||||reg||1
Z||000012||GT Bastard||03-16-2001||06:21 PM||||I also fastened  manifold wrapping around the slave cylinder to protect it from the heat off the headers better.  I wanted to build a heat shield, but never got around to it.||||reg||1
Z||000013||ray b||03-16-2001||08:17 PM||||my 88 formula shifts perfectly with no grinding, is smooth but car has only 21K mls.<BR>sounds like time to renew the master and slave cyl. as nothing lasts forever.||||reg||1
Z||000014||William Federle||03-16-2001||08:39 PM||||After I replaced my clutch pedal with a steel one, I replaced the clutch master cylinder in my 1984 Fiero when I found that if you held the pedal to the floor the slave extended but then slowly retracted. Someone at work said that it sounded like the brake fluid was leaking past the seal in the master cylinder. No leaking to the outside, just from one side of the piston to the other. When I got both the pedal and the master cylinder replaced, the slave would extend about 1-1/8 inches with full pedal travel and would stay extended when the clutch was held in. That was about 1 year ago and it's still working.<P>The biggest problem that I had was adapting the clutch pedal to the banjo of the new<BR>master cylinder. I found a brass bushing at ACE hardware with the right OD and ID and a thin shoulder.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||daveml66||03-16-2001||11:17 PM||||ya know what i feel a little better about my standard fiero now.  but just one other un-clutch related question, does neone know why my back brakes would be locking up??||||reg||6
Z||000016||85Josh||03-16-2001||11:34 PM||||Check your e-brake cables, mine were stuck when I got the car. I just disconnected them, I'm still scrounging cash for a total rebuild.<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4<BR>CAVALRY SCOUT!(stuck in Field Artillery)||||reg||1
Z||000017||Richard Parnell||03-18-2001||04:53 AM||||You're not listening folks.  It's not the slave, master or pedal.  The problem is that the Fiero has a crappy clutch.  Find a good after market non stock clutch and you will see what I'm talking about.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Red Dwarf||03-18-2001||10:26 AM||||Have a look at the lever that sticks out of the clutch housing, it is made from two pieces of steel and spot welded. On my 85 SE, the weld broke and the lever twisted every time the clutch was pressed.. I had exactly the same symptoms as you. Get someone to press the pedal while you have a look.<BR>||||reg||1
